Abstract
This project demonstrates a beginner-friendly machine-learning workflow for identifying network-flow records that resemble suspicious activity. A Random Forest classifier is trained to distinguish two labels, normal and attack, using eight numeric features. The application generates a reproducible synthetic dataset locally, splits it into training and test sets, evaluates held-out predictions, and saves metrics, plots, a CSV, and a trained model. The data is synthetic and is not captured from a live network. Consequently, the scores demonstrate the workflow on generated patterns only; they do not establish real-world detection performance.

1. Introduction
Network intrusion detection seeks to identify activity that may indicate unauthorized access, scanning, denial-of-service behavior, or other misuse. Machine-learning systems learn statistical relationships from labeled examples. This project introduces the supervised-learning workflow in a controlled, offline environment.

2. Problem Statement
Build a reproducible Python application that classifies flow-like records as normal or attack-like, reports standard metrics on held-out data, and saves artifacts for inspection and repeatability.

3. Objectives
• Generate a deterministic synthetic dataset with normal and attack-like labels.
• Use a stratified train/test split.
• Train a Random Forest classifier within a preprocessing pipeline.
• Measure accuracy, attack precision, attack recall, F1-score, and a confusion matrix.
• Save metrics, visualizations, generated data, and the trained model.
• Explain safety boundaries, limitations, and future work.
4. Scope and Safety
The code generates synthetic records locally. It does not perform packet capture, scanning, exploitation, or network requests. Attack-like rows are statistical patterns invented for this exercise, not verified records of malicious activity. This is an educational prototype, not a deployable security control. Any real traffic collection must be authorized and follow privacy and retention requirements.

