9. How to Run
1. Install Python 3.10 or newer.
2. Optionally create a virtual environment: python -m venv .venv, then activate it.
3. Install dependencies: python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
4. Run: python Adithya_R_Nair_ML_Network_Intrusion_Detector.py
5. Inspect the printed metrics and the results/ directory.
Optional arguments: --samples 2000 changes the generated row count (minimum 200); --output-dir results chooses the output directory.

10. Limitations
• The dataset is synthetic, not a public benchmark or real network traffic.
• Generated attack-like patterns do not cover the diversity of actual attacks.
• Scores may be inflated because generated patterns can be easy to distinguish.
• No live data ingestion, streaming inference, alert delivery, or analyst review is implemented.
• Feature importance reflects this model and dataset; it does not prove causation.
• Do not deploy this model to make security decisions without independent evaluation.
11. Future Improvements
• Evaluate on a documented and appropriately licensed public dataset such as CICIDS2017 or UNSW-NB15.
• Record dataset provenance, preprocessing choices, licensing, class imbalance, and leakage checks.
• Compare multiple models and tune thresholds using a validation set.
• Report false-positive rate and per-class metrics across different attack families.
• Add validated CSV inference and clear input/error handling.
• Assess concept drift, explainability, monitoring, and human review before any operational use.
12. Conclusion
This project demonstrates data generation, preprocessing, supervised model training, held-out evaluation, and artifact creation in Python. It is a reproducible learning prototype. Because the data is synthetic and the observed scores are perfect on the generated test split, further evaluation on a realistic, properly documented dataset is essential before drawing conclusions about real-world intrusion detection.

